  "id": 203711649697601,
  "uuid": "1eb931bc-4ea4-4d33-9710-761b12df1733",
  "is_folder": false,
  "content": {
    "_uid": "3ecf8da7-ea05-827b-a178-76b0d79bb0f4",
    "body": [
      {
        "_uid": "c526d968-a903-74d6-ecfd-0e221b065ea0",
        "content": {
          "type": "doc",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "A significant outcome of the conference was the launch of Pakistan Educational Research Association (PERA) on the final day of the conference. The launching ceremony was attended by a large group of educationists, academics and researchers from private and government institutions, including Dr Hafiz Iqbal, Director, Institute of Education and Research, University of Punjab; and Prof Noor-ul-Haq, Director, Provincial Institute of Teacher Education (PITE), Balochistan.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Speaking at the launching ceremony of PERA, AKU-IED Director Dr Muhammad Memon described it as a 'community of researchers'. Noting that promotion of research and scholarship was part of AKU's strategic vision, he said: \"It is in fulfilment of this vision that the University is committed to support the creation of PERA which would be a national resource in educational research for Pakistan.\"",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "The Head of Research and Policy Studies at AKU-IED, Dr Anjum Halai, provided an overview of and rationale for PERA. She said that PERA aimed to sustain and promote a vibrant educational research culture in Pakistan; enhance the impact of research on policy and practice; provide a forum for educationists and educational researchers to share research findings and best practices; disseminate research findings and encourage scholarly inquiry; and provide opportunities for members to build and enhance their research capacity. Although AKU-IED would support PERA in the initial phase, it would ultimately become an independent body with membership representing public and private sectors, she added.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "The conference also resulted in a set of papers that will be compiled as conference proceedings. This would be a significant resource as it would document a large number of papers on the topic of quality education with specific reference to issues pertaining to Pakistan and the developing world.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "AKU's academic, research and service activities - encompassing ten teaching sites in seven countries of South Asia, Eastern Africa and Europe - are targeted at helping the developing world, and especially the Muslim Ummah, to progress in areas of education and health.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
